## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: Severity-Ranked Findings

This test suite is a textbook example of **"coverage theater"** — every production method is invoked (achieving line coverage) but almost nothing is actually verified. Here's the ranked breakdown:

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests That Prove Nothing

**1. Missing outcome assertions (8 of 10 tests)**

| Test | Anti-Pattern | What could silently break |
|------|-------------|--------------------------|
| `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | No assert at all | `Save` could be a no-op |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33) | No assert at all | Price might not change, or wrong product saved |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39) | No assert at all | Product could still exist after "delete" |
| `GetProductCount_Works` (L64) | No assert at all | Returns 0 on empty catalog — never tested with data |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L69) | No assert at all | Prices could be unchanged or corrupted |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85) | No assert at all | Products might not actually persist |

**Fix pattern — assert the *effect*, not just non-null:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    var result = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", result!.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, result.Price);
}

[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesStoredPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
}

[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_MakesProductUnfindable()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}

[TestMethod]
public void ApplyBulkDiscount_ReducesPricesByFraction()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 100m));
    _catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
    Assert.AreEqual(90m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
}
```

---

### 🟠 High — Weak Assertions That Always Pass

**2. `Assert.IsNotNull` on value types / guaranteed non-null returns (L29, L51, L59, L81)**

`GetAll()` and `Search()` return `List<T>` — *never* null. `ExportToCsv()` returns `string` — *never* null. These asserts literally cannot fail.

**Fix:** Assert on content, not existence:
